{"records":[{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"mmnwww6n3s","title":"LZTFL1 Rewires NADPH–Glutathione Metabolism to Amplify Ferroptosis","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent form of regulated cell death, represents a therapeutic vulnerability in apoptosis-resistant cancers. Here, we identify leucine zipper transcription factor-like 1 (LZTFL1) as a key regulator of ferroptosis that rewires glutathione (GSH) metabolism. A genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 screen and multi-omics analysis revealed that LZTFL1 promotes oxidation of glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD), limiting NADPH production and GSH regeneration. GSH depletion in turn enhanced LZTFL1 translation via an AKT–mTOR–eIF4E pathway, establishing a feed-forward loop that amplifies ferroptosis. In vivo, the LZTFL1–FHOD1–G6PD axis sensitized xenograft and patient-derived xenograft models to ferroptosis, correlating with lipid peroxidation, reduced GSH, and extended survival. Clinically, LZTFL1 expression restored cisplatin efficacy in resistant lung and ovarian cancer cells and predicted better survival outcomes in LUAD patients. Moreover, FDA-approved compounds upregulated LZTFL1 and re-sensitized resistant tumors to cisplatin. These findings define LZTFL1 as both a biomarker and therapeutic target for enhancing ferroptosis-based cancer therapy. The files below contain raw western blot images and flow cytometry data from this study.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-12T06:59:22.445Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/mmnwww6n3s","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/mmnwww6n3s"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"XIANGFEI","last_name":"XUE","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Flowmetry"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r5ftnf4j9f","title":"Protein and Ligand Dataset for Drug Repositioning in Childhood Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL)","description":"This dataset includes two main components (proteins and ligands), which can be used in computational research focused on drug repositioning in Childhood Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL):\n\n1- Protein Sequences (proteins.txt): This dataset file contains amino acid sequences of selected proteins used in a study aiming to identify novel therapeutic candidates by drug repositioning for Childhood Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia (ALL). The sequences were extracted from the UniProt database and are proteins which are known or predicted to be associated with ALL pathogenesis, treatment procedures, or immunological relevance. The file is structured in a JSON-like format with UniProt IDs as keys and amino acid sequences as values. Each entry corresponds to one protein. \nAdditional Metadata:\n- Data Type: Amino acid sequence data (FASTA-like JSON format)\n- Unique Proteins: 8479\n- Average Sequence Length: 529.81\n- Maximum Sequence Length: 14507\n\n2. Ligand Data (ligands.txt): A collection of drug-like small molecules represented in SMILES or similar formats. The ligands in this file were selected by considering their therapeutic potential and relevance to ALL-related targets. The data was sourced from databases such as ChEMBL and DrugBank. Also, some data related to FDA approved drugs were added manually. \nAdditional Metadata:\n- Data Type: SMILES strings in JSON-like format (key-value pairs).\n- Number of ligands: ~220.000\n\nThe combined dataset supports research in bioinformatics, drug discovery, and leukemia-specific therapeutic targeting.\n\nThe dataset is designed to aid computational biology, bioinformatics and artificial intelligence research, especially for researchers in the field of leukemia biology, drug-target interaction modeling, and systems pharmacology.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-04-21T23:08:13.99Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r5ftnf4j9f","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r5ftnf4j9f"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Utku","last_name":"KÖSE","orcid":"0000-0002-9652-6415"},{"first_name":"Ilhan","last_name":"Uysal","orcid":"0000-0002-6091-9110"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/04w9kkr77","name":"Scientific and Technological Research Council of Turkey","grant_id":"123E383"}],"categories":["Pharmacology","Artificial Intelligence","Bioinformatics","Protein","Ligand","Deep Learning","Drug Repositioning","Childhood Acute Lymphocytic Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"gjxh7wrycw","title":"Exploiting The ASNS Vulnerability Gap: Five Decades of Selective Metabolic Starvation in Oncology","description":"This repository contains the Python scripts, source data, and visualization code used to generate the figures and diagrams featured in the comprehensive review manuscript titled \"Exploiting The ASNS Vulnerability Gap: Five Decades of Selective Metabolic Starvation in Oncology\" (or \"L-asparaginase in Oncology: Metabolic Vulnerabilities and Therapeutic Evolution\").\nThe dataset provides reproducible workflows for bibliometric trend analyses, text-mining term frequency calculations, therapeutic comparative matrices, and decision-pathway diagrams related to L-asparaginase (L-ASNase) research in acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL), extranodal NK/T-cell lymphoma (ENKTCL), and solid tumors.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-09-15T11:01:42.105Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/gjxh7wrycw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/gjxh7wrycw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Kh Abdullah","last_name":"Al-Aff","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Galib Muhammad Shahriar","last_name":"Himel","orcid":"0000-0002-2257-6751"},{"first_name":"Doaa Khalid","last_name":"Mezaal","orcid":"0009-0000-0333-7387"},{"first_name":"Kunnummal","last_name":"Sumithlal","orcid":"0009-0007-5282-9338"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Pharmacology","Oncology","Cancer Metabolism"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"55v76yw529","title":"Deubiquitinases: Pro-oncogenic Activity and Therapeutic Targeting in Acute Leukemia","description":"Dysregulation of kinase signaling pathways via mutations favors tumor cell survival and resistance to therapy and it is common in cancer. In our work we reveal a novel mechanism of posttranslational regulation of kinase signaling and nuclear receptor activity via deubiquitination in acute leukemia. We observed that the ubiquitin specific protease 7 (USP7) forms a complex with USP11 to deubiquitinate oncogenic lymphocyte cell-specific protein tyrosine kinase (LCK). Deubiquitination of LCK controls its activity, thereby altering T cell receptor signaling. Impairment of LCK activity leads to increased expression of the glucocorticoid receptor (GR) transcript, culminating into transcriptional activation of pro-apoptotic target genes and sensitizes cells to glucocorticoids in primary T cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia (T-ALL) patient samples. The transcriptional activation of pro-apoptotic target genes, such as BCL2L11, is orchestrated by the deubiquitinase activity and mediated via an increase in enhancer-promoter interaction intensity. Our data unveil how dysregulated deubiquitination controls signaling pathways, leading to cancer cell survival and drug non-response, and suggest novel therapeutic combinations towards targeting leukemia.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-16T20:51:22.317Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/55v76yw529","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/55v76yw529"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Blanca","last_name":"Gutierrez","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Biochemistry","Protein-Tyrosine Kinase","Ubiquitylation","Acute Leukemia"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"66kbk28dzh","title":"Ph+ALL and P2RX1","description":"raw data of P2RX1 promotes mitochondrial apoptosis via calcium/CaM KII-mediated suppression of PI3K/Akt signaling in Philadelphia chromosome-positive acute lymphoblastic leukemia","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-12-05T00:23:18.073Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/66kbk28dzh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/66kbk28dzh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"xiao bing","last_name":"li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Experimental Design"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":121298,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":173,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":121298,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":120755,"restricted":61,"embargoed":482},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2080},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":604},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":405},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":398},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":393},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":361},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":307},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":241},{"id":"https://ror.org/00k4n6c32","name":"European Commission","count":211}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":118722},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":175},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":119},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":74},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":65},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":53},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":35},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e